**Phase 1 — Sequence:** The fragment’s inputs are the parameters `size`, `offset`, and `*max` (`u32`), plus the page-cache reads. After `len = *max; if (!len) return size;` (lines 10-12), `len` has residual range `1..UINT_MAX`; `size` and `offset` are left unconstrained by any in-fragment validation, though line 55 uses `size` as the bitmap-end boundary in the first search loop. The return value of `read_mapping_page` is not checked; I do not rely on that because no NULL/error producer is visible in-fragment. The security-relevant operations are the page reads at lines 57 and 104, the in-page pointer dereferences lines 28/42/91/120, and the allocation-length arithmetic lines 76/98-99. In particular, the second page-advance path (lines 101-108) lacks the `offset >= size` bound present in the first search loop (lines 54-56), and the boundary condition at line 92 (`if (len <    32)`) admits `len == 32` into the full-word path.

**Phase 2 — Branch:** Line 92 is the decisive guard. It tests `len` directly against the same `len` consumed at line 99 (`len -=    32`), so there is no type/precision drift; the only interleaved mutation is the decrement itself, which is exactly the off-by-one fold. `len <    32` admits `len == 32`; that value then takes the full-word path: line 98 writes the word with `*curr++`, and line 99 sets `len` to 0. If that word was the last word in the final page, `curr` becomes `end`, the inner loop at line 90 cannot continue, and control falls to lines 101-103. Line 55’s `offset >= size` guard is not on this allocation path; it appears only in the earlier “scan complete u32s” loop and does not cover this sink. Line 71’s `start >= size` check excludes `start == size`, but does not exclude the boundary condition that makes `len == 32` at the last page word. The absent one-statement fix is therefore changing line 92 to `if (len <=    32)` (or equivalently terminating allocation at this boundary), which would send `len == 32` to `last:` and write the final word inside the mapped page without advancing `curr` to `end`.

**Phase 3 — Loop:** In the “do full u32s” outer loop (lines 89-109), the inner loop terminates only when `curr >= end`. When the remaining allocation is exactly the last 32-bit word of a page-aligned bitmap, filling that word advances `curr` to `end`, then lines 101-103 execute. Line 103 `offset += PAGE_CACHE_BITS` makes `offset == size` exactly;because the second loop has no `offset >= size` check, line 104 reads a page at `offset / PAGE_CACHE_BITS` beyond the bitmap delimited by `size`. The loop does not terminate safely at this boundary; it continues into a one-page out-of-range access.

**Phase 4 — Confirmation:** Concrete witness: with `PAGE_CACHE_BITS = P`, let `size = P`, `offset = 0`, requested `*max >= 64`, and the bitmap’s last two `__be32` words being free while the preceding bits are allocated. The scan finds `start = P - 64`; line 76 sets `len = min(64, *max) = 64`. The first partial word consumes 32 bits and leaves `len = 32`(lines 77-87). Line 92 admits that remaining `len == 32`; line 98 fills the last word and `len -=  32` makes it zero; `curr` becomes `end`. Then line 103 advances `offset` to `P == size`, and line 104 calls `read_mapping_page` with `offset / PAGE_CACHE_BITS =1`, one page past the bitmap covered by `size`; this is then dereferenced/written at lines 91/120. This is a confirmed out-of-range read/write of a page beyond the bitmap datum; the missing one-line fix is the changed comparison at line 92 (`<` should be `<=`), whose excluded boundary value is exactly `len == 32`. Primary CWE: CWE-125; severity high (kernel mount-time access from a crafted filesystem can read/dirty an out-of-range page and crash/corrupt state). Confidence is calibrated at 0.74 because the value chain is exact but the provenance of `*max`/`size` (helper parameters with no shown call site) and the page-cache behavior beyond EOF are partially assumed.

